\lessonlabel{2}
\section{Number Systems and Codes}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item write numbers in binary, octal and hexadecimal forms;
  \item convert between decimal, binary, octal and hexadecimal;
  \item explain the purpose of binary‑coded decimal (BCD);
  \item perform simple BCD conversions.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Binary system}

The \term{binary} system uses only two digits, 0 and 1. Each position represents a power of 2, starting with $2^{0}$ at the right‑most digit.

For example, the binary number $1011_2$ equals:
\[
1\cdot2^{3}+0\cdot2^{2}+1\cdot2^{1}+1\cdot2^{0}=8+0+2+1=11_{10}.
\]

\begin{example}
Convert $11010_2$ to decimal.
\[
1\cdot2^{4}+1\cdot2^{3}+0\cdot2^{2}+1\cdot2^{1}+0\cdot2^{0}=16+8+0+2+0=26_{10}.
\]
\end{example}

\subsection{Octal system}

The \term{octal} system uses digits 0–7. Each position represents a power of 8.

For instance, $157_8$ equals:
\[
1\cdot8^{2}+5\cdot8^{1}+7\cdot8^{0}=64+40+7=111_{10}.
\]

\begin{example}
Convert $73_8$ to decimal.
\[
7\cdot8^{1}+3\cdot8^{0}=56+3=59_{10}.
\]
\end{example}

\subsection{Hexadecimal system}

The \term{hexadecimal} system uses sixteen symbols: 0–9 and A–F (where A=10, …, F=15). Each position represents a power of 16.

Example: $2B3_{16}$ equals:
\[
2\cdot16^{2}+11\cdot16^{1}+3\cdot16^{0}=512+176+3=691_{10}.
\]

\begin{example}
Convert $1F_{16}$ to decimal.
\[
1\cdot16^{1}+15\cdot16^{0}=16+15=31_{10}.
\]
\end{example}

\subsection{Conversions between bases}

\subsubsection{Decimal to binary}

Repeatedly divide by 2, recording remainders. The binary digits are the remainders read upward.

\begin{example}
Convert $45_{10}$ to binary.
\[
45/2=22\text{ r }1,\;22/2=11\text{ r }0,\;11/2=5\text{ r }1,\;5/2=2\text{ r }1,\;2/2=1\text{ r }0,\;1/2=0\text{ r }1.
\]
Reading remainders upward gives $101101_2$.
\end{example}

\subsubsection{Binary to octal}

Group binary digits in threes from the right; replace each group by its octal value.

\begin{example}
Convert $1101101_2$ to octal.
\[
110\;110\;1\underline{000}\rightarrow 6\;6\;0_{8}=660_{8}.
\]
(Added leading zeros to make a full group of three.)
\end{example}

\subsubsection{Binary to hexadecimal}

Group binary digits in fours from the right; replace each group by its hexadecimal value.

\begin{example}
Convert $10111011_2$ to hexadecimal.
\[
1011\;1011\rightarrow B\;B_{16}=BB_{16}.
\]
\end{example}

\subsubsection{Octal to binary and hexadecimal}

Convert each octal digit to a three‑bit binary group, then apply the binary‑to‑hex method if needed.

\begin{example}
Convert $57_8$ to hexadecimal.
\[
5_8=101_2,\;7_8=111_2\;\Rightarrow\;101111_2.
\]
Group as $0010\;1111$ → $2F_{16}$.
\end{example}

\subsection{Binary‑coded decimal (BCD)}

\term{Binary‑coded decimal} stores each decimal digit separately as a four‑bit binary number. For example, the decimal number 93 is represented as $1001\;0011_{BCD}$ (9 → 1001, 3 → 0011).

BCD is useful in digital displays because each digit can be driven independently.

\begin{example}
Encode $254$ in BCD.
\[
2\rightarrow0010,\;5\rightarrow0101,\;4\rightarrow0100\;\Rightarrow\;0010\;0101\;0100_{BCD}.
\]
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Convert $101101_2$ to octal, hexadecimal and decimal. Then write the decimal result in BCD.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Write $27_{10}$ in binary, octal and hexadecimal.
  \item Convert $3F_{16}$ to binary and then to octal.
  \item Encode the decimal number $718$ in BCD.
  \item Decode the BCD value $0100\;1001\;0010_{BCD}$ to a decimal number.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Binary & Base‑2 numeral system using digits 0 and 1. \\
Octal & Base‑8 numeral system using digits 0–7. \\
Hexadecimal & Base‑16 numeral system using digits 0–9 and A–F. \\
Conversion & Process of changing a number from one base to another. \\
Binary‑coded decimal (BCD) & Representation of each decimal digit by a separate 4‑bit binary code. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{3}
\section{Boolean Algebra}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item state the basic Boolean laws;
  \item construct truth tables for Boolean expressions;
  \item apply the laws to simplify Boolean expressions.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Boolean laws}

The following identities hold for any Boolean variables $A$, $B$, $C$.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Identity laws}: $A+0 = A$, $A\cdot 1 = A$.
  \item \term{Null laws}: $A+1 = 1$, $A\cdot 0 = 0$.
  \item \term{Idempotent laws}: $A+A = A$, $A\cdot A = A$.
  \item \term{Complement laws}: $A+ \overline{A}=1$, $A\cdot \overline{A}=0$.
  \item \term{Commutative laws}: $A+B = B+A$, $A\cdot B = B\cdot A$.
  \item \term{Associative laws}: $(A+B)+C = A+(B+C)$, $(A\cdot B)\cdot C = A\cdot (B\cdot C)$.
  \item \term{Distributive laws}: $A\cdot (B+ C)=A\cdot B + A\cdot C$, $A+ (B\cdot C)= (A+ B)\cdot (A+ C)$.
  \item \term{De Morgan's laws}: $\overline{A+ B}= \overline{A}\cdot \overline{B}$, $\overline{A\cdot B}= \overline{A}+ \overline{B}$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
Simplify $A\cdot 1 + A\cdot \overline{A}$.
\[
A\cdot 1 + A\cdot \overline{A}= A + A\cdot \overline{A}= A(1+\overline{A})=A\cdot 1 = A.
\]
The identity law $A\cdot1=A$ and the complement law $1+\overline{A}=1$ were used.
\end{example}

\subsection{Truth tables}

A truth table lists the output of a Boolean expression for every combination of its input variables.

\begin{example}
Truth table for $F = A + \overline{B}$.

\[
\begin{array}{c c | c}
A & B & F \\ \hline
0 & 0 & 0 + 1 = 1 \\
0 & 1 & 0 + 0 = 0 \\
1 & 0 & 1 + 1 = 1 \\
1 & 1 & 1 + 0 = 1 \\
\end{array}
\]
\end{example}

\subsection{Simplifying expressions}

To simplify a Boolean expression, repeatedly apply the Boolean laws until no further reduction is possible.

\begin{example}
Simplify $F = \overline{A\cdot B}+A\cdot B$.

\[
\begin{aligned}
F &= \overline{A\cdot B}+A\cdot B \\
  &= (\overline{A}+ \overline{B}) + A\cdot B \quad\text{(De Morgan)}\\
  &= (\overline{A}+ \overline{B}+A)\cdot(\overline{A}+ \overline{B}+B) \quad\text{(Distributive)}\\
  &= (1+ \overline{B})\cdot(\overline{A}+1) \quad\text{(Complement, Identity)}\\
  &= 1\cdot 1 = 1.
\end{aligned}
\]
Thus $F$ simplifies to the constant $1$.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Reduce the expression $G = (A+ B)\cdot (\overline{A}+ B)\cdot (A+ \overline{B})$ using the Boolean laws. Show each step.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Write the truth table for $H = \overline{A}\cdot B + A\cdot \overline{B}$.
  \item Using the Boolean laws, simplify $J = (A+0)\cdot (B+1)$.
  \item Prove De Morgan's first law $\overline{A+ B}= \overline{A}\cdot \overline{B}$ by constructing a truth table.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{p{3.5cm}p{11.5cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\ \hline
Boolean law & An identity that holds for all Boolean variables (e.g., identity, complement). \\ 
Truth table & A tabular method that lists the output of a Boolean expression for every input combination. \\ 
Simplification & The process of reducing a Boolean expression to a shorter equivalent form using the laws. \\ 
De Morgan's laws & Two transformation rules that relate complements of sums and products. \\ 
\hline
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{4}
\section{Logic Gates}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item describe the function of the basic logic gates AND, OR, NOT, NAND, NOR and XOR;
  \item draw the symbol for each gate;
  \item write the truth table for each gate;
  \item combine gates to implement simple Boolean expressions.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{AND gate}

The \term{AND} gate outputs 1 only when all its inputs are 1. Its symbol is a flat‑sided rectangle with a curved back.

\[
\text{output}=A\land B
\]

\begin{example}
For inputs $A=1$, $B=0$ the AND gate gives $1\land0=0$. The output is 0.
\end{example}

\subsection{OR gate}

The \term{OR} gate outputs 1 when at least one input is 1. Its symbol is a curved shape pointing to the right.

\[
\text{output}=A\lor B
\]

\begin{example}
With $A=0$, $B=1$ the OR gate gives $0\lor1=1$. The output is 1.
\end{example}

\subsection{NOT gate}

The \term{NOT} gate (inverter) has one input and produces the opposite logical value.

\[
\text{output}= \lnot A
\]

\begin{example}
If $A=0$ then $\lnot A =1$. The output is 1.
\end{example}

\subsection{NAND gate}

The \term{NAND} gate is the negation of AND. It outputs 0 only when all inputs are 1.

\[
\text{output}= \lnot (A\land B)
\]

\begin{example}
For $A=1$, $B=1$ the NAND gate gives $\lnot(1\land1)=\lnot1=0$. The output is 0.
\end{example}

\subsection{NOR gate}

The \term{NOR} gate is the negation of OR. It outputs 1 only when all inputs are 0.

\[
\text{output}= \lnot (A\lor B)
\]

\begin{example}
With $A=0$, $B=0$ the NOR gate gives $\lnot(0\lor0)=\lnot0=1$. The output is 1.
\end{example}

\subsection{XOR gate}

The \term{XOR} (exclusive OR) gate outputs 1 when exactly one input is 1.

\[
\text{output}= A\oplus B
\]

\begin{example}
If $A=1$, $B=0$ then $A\oplus B =1$. The output is 1. If both are 1, the output is 0.
\end{example}

\subsection{Building circuits from gates}

Complex Boolean functions can be built by connecting basic gates. For example, the expression $F = (A\land B) \lor (\lnot C)$ can be realised with one AND gate, one NOT gate, and one OR gate. The output of the AND gate and the NOT gate are fed into the OR gate.

\begin{example}
Implement $F = (A\land B) \lor (\lnot C)$.  
1. Connect $A$ and $B$ to an AND gate.  
2. Connect $C$ to a NOT gate.  
3. Connect the outputs of the AND and NOT gates to an OR gate.  
The final output of the OR gate is $F$.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Draw the circuit that implements $G = \lnot (A\lor B) \land (C\oplus D)$. Identify each gate used.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Write the truth table for a two‑input NAND gate.
  \item Simplify the expression $H = \lnot(A\land B) \lor (A\land \lnot B)$ using gate symbols.
  \item Design a circuit that outputs 1 only when exactly two of the three inputs $X$, $Y$, $Z$ are 1. Indicate the gates required.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{l l}
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
AND & Outputs 1 only if all inputs are 1. \\
OR & Outputs 1 if any input is 1. \\
NOT & Inverts a single input. \\
NAND & Negated AND; outputs 0 only when all inputs are 1. \\
NOR & Negated OR; outputs 1 only when all inputs are 0. \\
XOR & Outputs 1 when an odd number of inputs are 1. \\
Circuit & Arrangement of gates that implements a Boolean function. \\
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{5}
\section{Combinational Circuits}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item describe the operation of a half‑adder and a full‑adder;
  \item construct a $2$‑to‑1 multiplexer and explain its truth table;
  \item design a binary decoder and state its applications;
  \item explain how an encoder performs the inverse mapping of a decoder;
  \item analyse simple combinational networks for correct output.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Adders}

A \term{half‑adder} adds two single binary digits $A$ and $B$. It produces a \textit{sum} $S$ and a \textit{carry} $C$. The logic equations are $S = A \oplus B$ and $C = A \cdot B$.

A \term{full‑adder} adds three bits: two data bits $A$, $B$ and an incoming carry $C_{\text{in}}$. It yields a sum $S$ and a carry out $C_{\text{out}}$ with
\[
S = A \oplus B \oplus C_{\text{in}},\qquad
C_{\text{out}} = (A\cdot B) + (B\cdot C_{\text{in}}) + (A\cdot C_{\text{in}}).
\]

\begin{example}
Add $A=1$, $B=1$, $C_{\text{in}}=0$ using a full‑adder.  
$S = 1 \oplus 1 \oplus 0 = 0$, $C_{\text{out}} = (1\cdot1)+(1\cdot0)+(1\cdot0)=1$.  
Result is binary $10$, i.e., $2$ in decimal.
\end{example}

\subsection{Multiplexers}

A \term{multiplexer} (or \term{mux}) selects one of several input lines and forwards it to a single output line, based on selector bits. A $2$‑to‑1 mux has inputs $D_0$, $D_1$, selector $S$, and output $Y$ with
\[
Y = \overline{S}\,D_0 + S\,D_1.
\]

\begin{example}
Let $D_0=0$, $D_1=1$, and selector $S=1$. Then $Y = \overline{1}\cdot0 + 1\cdot1 = 1$. The mux passes $D_1$ to the output.
\end{example}

\subsection{Decoders}

A \term{decoder} converts a binary code into a one‑hot output. A $2$‑to‑4 decoder has two selector inputs $A_1A_0$ and four outputs $Y_0$–$Y_3$, where $Y_i = 1$ only when the binary input equals $i$.

\begin{example}
For input $A_1A_0 = 10$ (binary $2$), the decoder sets $Y_2 = 1$ and all other $Y_i = 0$.
\end{example}

\subsection{Encoders}

An \term{encoder} performs the opposite mapping of a decoder: it receives a one‑hot input and produces the binary code of the active line. A $4$‑to‑2 encoder has inputs $D_3\!-\!D_0$ and outputs $A_1A_0$, where $A_1A_0$ is the binary index of the high input.

\begin{example}
If $D_2 = 1$ and all other $D_i = 0$, the encoder outputs $A_1A_0 = 10$ (binary $2$).
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Design a circuit that adds two 2‑bit numbers using full‑adders, and verify its output for the input pair $01 + 11$.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Draw the truth table for a half‑adder and a full‑adder.
  \item Implement a $2$‑to‑1 mux using only NAND gates.
  \item Write the Boolean expressions for the outputs of a $3$‑to‑8 decoder.
  \item Given a one‑hot input $D_0=0$, $D_1=0$, $D_2=1$, $D_3=0$, state the encoder output.
  \item Combine two full‑adders to create a 2‑bit adder and compute the sum of $10_2$ and $01_2$.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Half‑adder & Circuit that adds two single bits, producing sum and carry. \\
Full‑adder & Circuit that adds three bits (two data bits and a carry‑in). \\
Multiplexer & Device that selects one of many inputs based on selector bits. \\
Decoder & Circuit that converts binary input to a one‑hot output pattern. \\
Encoder & Circuit that converts a one‑hot input to its binary index. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
